message.content:
{
  "42": {
    "document_id": "42",
    "label": "1",
    "explanation": "Lott and Collins announce that they reached an agreement whose provisions were included in the Collins-Lieberman bill: 'The agreement between Senators Collins and Lott resulted in the following provisions being included in the Collins Lieberman legislation.' Under clarification (3), releases whose primary news is the senators' own legislative product — provisions they succeeded in including in advancing legislation — count as credit-claiming. The senators present themselves as the source of the FEMA-strengthening provisions."
  },
  "714": {
    "document_id": "714",
    "label": "1",
    "explanation": "Although the column is largely informational and congratulatory toward the U.S. Marshals, it contains an explicit first-person claim of a concrete past achievement: 'I helped secure more than $3 billion in the fiscal year 2006 Homeland Security appropriations bill for ICE's domestic enforcement efforts, as well as funding for 250 additional criminal investigators.' Under clarification (2) ('helped secure') and (5) (featured recitation of funding previously won), and mirroring the Durbin positive example where a first-person 'helped secure' claim made an otherwise advocacy/commendation piece credit-claiming, this is coded 1. I considered the boundary rule about passing references, but this is a specific, substantive funding claim tied to the column's subject rather than mere biographical filler."
  },
  "794": {
    "document_id": "794",
    "label": "1",
    "explanation": "While the release is largely a position-taking update to local officials (Iraq, taxes, energy), it features a concrete credit claim: 'The new federal highway bill, which Bond co-authored, is providing Missouri with much-needed resources, including $1 billion in new money — more than $4 billion in total funding.' Under the definition (legislation the senator authored) and clarification (5) (featured recitation of concrete past achievements for constituents — funding previously won), this specific claim of responsibility for billions delivered to Missouri is a featured element, not a passing biographical note. Coded 1."
  },
  "805": {
    "document_id": "805",
    "label": "1",
    "explanation": "Shelby, identified with role language as 'a member of the Senate Appropriations Committee,' announces that DHS will release $3,510 to the Homewood Fire Department. Under clarification (1), announcements of federal money benefiting the senator's state count as credit-claiming even without an explicit 'I secured it,' because the senator serving as bearer of the news functions as a claim of responsibility."
  },
  "1257": {
    "document_id": "1257",
    "label": "1",
    "explanation": "Paradigmatic credit-claiming. DeWine announces a $105,550 grant to the Parma Fire Department delivered 'through Sen. DeWine's bill'; the release states he 'wrote and passed the FIRE Act,' 'helped secure approximately $650 million for the FIRE Act,' and passed an updated version. This matches clarification (1) (announcement of federal funds to the state) and (2) (explicit claims: 'helped secure,' 'wrote and passed'), like the Talent positive example."
  },
  "1325": {
    "document_id": "1325",
    "label": "0",
    "explanation": "This is a floor statement on the judicial nominations process that criticizes Democrats' filibusters and defends the constitutional option. It is pure position-taking and criticism of colleagues (negative clarification (1) and (2)); Dole claims no responsibility for any government action or output."
  },
  "1749": {
    "document_id": "1749",
    "label": "1",
    "explanation": "The release's primary news is Hutchison signing on as an original cosponsor of the Veterans Housing Benefits Enhancement Act. Under clarification (3), releases whose primary news is the senator's own legislative product — introducing, sponsoring, or cosponsoring a bill — count as credit-claiming."
  },
  "1897": {
    "document_id": "1897",
    "label": "0",
    "explanation": "Martinez's statement sets out his position on the Pombo OCS bill (opposition in current form, conditions for support) and praises Congressman Putnam's efforts. This is position-taking plus praise directed at others (negative clarifications (1) and (2)); he claims no responsibility for any delivered outcome."
  },
  "1927": {
    "document_id": "1927",
    "label": "1",
    "explanation": "Snowe announces $8.1 million in the FY2006 MilCon/VA appropriations bill for the Portsmouth Naval Shipyard and explicitly states 'This funding was requested by Snowe and Sen. Susan Collins,' plus a letter she sent requesting veterans funding. Under clarification (1) (announcement of federal money with role/request language) and (2) ('at my request'), this is credit-claiming."
  },
  "2335": {
    "document_id": "2335",
    "label": "0",
    "explanation": "The release's purpose is an essay contest honoring 'Outstanding Hoosier Dads' — ceremonial/publicity content expressly excluded by negative clarification (3) ('essay contests'). The references to Bayh's Responsible Fatherhood Act and gubernatorial record are passing biographical background supporting the contest theme; under the boundary rule, such references inside a ceremonial release are not sufficient for a '1.'"
  },
  "2869": {
    "document_id": "2869",
    "label": "0",
    "explanation": "Jeffords' statement simply explains his votes in support of the Kerry and Levin amendments on Iraq. Under negative clarification (1), explanations of votes and statements of support that claim no responsibility for an outcome are position-taking, not credit-claiming."
  },
  "3252": {
    "document_id": "3252",
    "label": "1",
    "explanation": "The headline news is that the Senate adopted Nelson's amendment: 'his amendment... passed on a bipartisan vote of 72 to 26,' and it is repeatedly called 'the Nelson amendment.' Under clarification (3), adoption of the senator's amendment in advancing legislation is credit-claiming."
  },
  "4022": {
    "document_id": "4022",
    "label": "1",
    "explanation": "Although much of the statement criticizes the administration's health care record, its primary news is Kennedy's own legislative product: 'Today I am introducing legislation to extend Medicare to all Americans... I call this approach Medicare for All.' Under clarification (3), releases whose primary news is the senator introducing a bill count as credit-claiming, presenting the senator as the source of a valued government action."
  },
  "4122": {
    "document_id": "4122",
    "label": "1",
    "explanation": "The release announces Coleman and Levin's hearing but prominently features quantified results of their subcommittee's oversight: 'Due to the Subcommittee's oversight, collections from delinquent Defense contractors skyrocketed by 4,415%... to $31 million in 2006 alone' and 'levies on payments to deadbeat federal contractors have jumped 746%, raking in an additional $122 million in back taxes.' Under clarification (4), claims that agency action resulted from the senators' oversight, including quantified results of their investigations such as funds recouped, are credit-claiming."
  },
  "4184": {
    "document_id": "4184",
    "label": "1",
    "explanation": "The headline and text center on Kyl's own legislative product: 'SEN. KYL-SPONSORED AMENDMENT,' 'Sen. Jon Kyl today co-sponsored legislation,' 'The Kyl amendment to the Senate Budget Resolution calls for $352 million.' Under clarification (3), releases whose primary news is the senator sponsoring or co-sponsoring a bill/amendment are credit-claiming."
  },
  "4207": {
    "document_id": "4207",
    "label": "1",
    "explanation": "The lead news is a letter urging Secretary Chertoff to consider the IBWC levee proposal (advocacy), but the release features a full paragraph of Cornyn's concrete past actions for South Texas: he 'added an amendment to the Department of State and Foreign Operations Appropriations bill that required the IBWC... to report to Congress' (an adopted provision) and pressed Appropriations for maximum levee funding. This mirrors the Durbin positive example, where an advocacy letter became credit-claiming because of a featured recitation of the senator's prior achievements (clarification (3) and (5))."
  },
  "4295": {
    "document_id": "4295",
    "label": "1",
    "explanation": "Murkowski's release announces she 'today introduced her second piece of legislation' to change Alaska's Medicaid FMAP formula (clarification (3): introducing a bill). It also features a past concrete achievement: she 'helped win a two-year change... that provided the state with an additional $34.11 million above the traditional Medicaid formula amount' (clarification (5), funding previously won). Both elements are credit-claiming."
  },
  "4508": {
    "document_id": "4508",
    "label": "1",
    "explanation": "Lott announces that FEMA is moving 300 travel trailers and 100 mobile homes to South Mississippi for Katrina victims, and the release explicitly ties the action to his intervention: 'The announcement follows the Senator's Monday appeal to President Bush to authorize the immediate transport of available trailers.' Under clarification (1) (announcement of federal resources benefiting the state) and (4) (an agency action that followed the senator's appeal), this is credit-claiming."
  },
  "4677": {
    "document_id": "4677",
    "label": "0",
    "explanation": "Hutchison questioned officials at a hearing, received assurances that BRAC is on schedule, 'expressed support' for the Army's budget request, and said she is 'encouraged to see $18 million in the emergency supplemental for facilities at Fort Hood.' The $18 million is part of the Army's budget/supplemental request, and she claims no role in producing it; the Fort Hood growth is prospective ('will be considered'). Under negative clarification (5), welcoming or applauding decisions made by others without claiming a role is not credit-claiming. Her 'I have supported increasing the Army's end strength' is general position support, not a claim of responsibility for a delivered output."
  },
  "4774": {
    "document_id": "4774",
    "label": "1",
    "explanation": "Although framed as letters urging conferees to act, the release's core is Bayh's own legislative product: it repeatedly calls the provision 'his legislation' and notes that 'the Senate unanimously approved an amendment offered by Senators Bayh and Richard Durbin.' Under clarification (3), adoption/inclusion of the senator's amendment in advancing legislation is credit-claiming, and the letters serve to demonstrate support for 'his legislation.' Coded 1."
  }
}

{
  "50": {
    "document_id": "50",
    "label": "0",
    "explanation": "Reid's statement criticizes the Bush administration's 'head in the sand' position on global warming. It is pure position-taking and criticism of others (negative clarification 1 and 2); Reid claims no responsibility for any government action or outcome."
  },
  "57": {
    "document_id": "57",
    "label": "1",
    "explanation": "The release's primary news is that Senator Kennedy introduced legislation (the Human Cloning Ban and Stem Cell Research Protection Act) with named co-sponsors. Per clarification (3), releases whose primary news is the senator's own legislative product — introducing, sponsoring, or cosponsoring a bill — count as credit-claiming, since the senator presents himself as the source of a valued government action."
  },
  "89": {
    "document_id": "89",
    "label": "1",
    "explanation": "Dorgan announces a federal designation (Bismarck State College as the National Power Plant Operations Technology and Educational Center) benefiting his state, and the release explicitly states 'Dorgan authored legislation, which was signed into law, that created the National Center designation.' This fits clarification (1) (announcement of a designation benefiting the state) and clarification (2) (explicit claim of authorship)."
  },
  "240": {
    "document_id": "240",
    "label": "0",
    "explanation": "Ensign's statement on the Chertoff confirmation praises the nominee ('He has dedicated his life to public service') and expresses intent to work with him. This is position-taking and praise directed at another (negative clarifications 1 and 2); no outcome or benefit is claimed as Ensign's doing."
  },
  "453": {
    "document_id": "453",
    "label": "0",
    "explanation": "Nelson explains his votes against two Iraq withdrawal resolutions and states his preference for measurable goals over arbitrary dates. This is a classic explanation of a vote/position-taking under negative clarification (1); he claims no responsibility for any government output."
  },
  "597": {
    "document_id": "597",
    "label": "0",
    "explanation": "The release covers Lugar presenting the Dick Lugar Health and Fitness Award at his annual community run/walk. Under negative clarification (3), award ceremonies (giving honors) and ceremonial/publicity content are excluded even when they recite the senator's biography; no government action or benefit is claimed."
  },
  "656": {
    "document_id": "656",
    "label": "0",
    "explanation": "This matches the codebook's negative example exactly: Kerry's statement on the Dubai ports deal is entirely an attack on the administration's port-security failures. Criticizing others is position-taking (negative clarification 1 and 2); he claims no role in producing any outcome."
  },
  "918": {
    "document_id": "918",
    "label": "0",
    "explanation": "This is a town-meeting announcement, explicitly excluded under negative clarification (3) as ceremonial/publicity content. The listing of Allard's committee memberships is incidental biographical information, not a claim of responsibility for any government output."
  },
  "967": {
    "document_id": "967",
    "label": "1",
    "explanation": "Shelby, identified as a member of the Appropriations Committee, announces that HHS will release $3,305,732 for Head Start programs in Talladega-area counties. Per clarification (1), announcements of federal money benefiting the senator's state count as credit-claiming even without an explicit 'I secured it'; the appropriations role language reinforces the implied claim of responsibility."
  },
  "1021": {
    "document_id": "1021",
    "label": "1",
    "explanation": "Wyden and Smith announce that the Senate Appropriations Committee has approved funding for the Pacific Coast Salmon Recovery Fund, Oregon State University ocean research, and numerous other Oregon-specific programs. Per clarification (1), senators serving as bearers of news about federal funds delivered to their state functions as a claim of responsibility for a valued government output."
  },
  "1957": {
    "document_id": "1957",
    "label": "1",
    "explanation": "Dodd and Lieberman announce that Bradley International Airport will receive a $2,250,000 federal grant for runway/taxiway improvements. Under clarification (1), announcements of federal grants benefiting the senator's state are credit-claiming, as the senators present themselves as the source/bearer of a valued government benefit."
  },
  "2318": {
    "document_id": "2318",
    "label": "1",
    "explanation": "This matches the codebook's positive example exactly. Although the letter urges state officials to act, Durbin explicitly states 'I helped secure an expedited environmental review from the federal government and facilitated the return of the project to the national priority list for federal funding' — a featured first-person claim of past results under clarifications (2) and (5)."
  },
  "2807": {
    "document_id": "2807",
    "label": "0",
    "explanation": "Lincoln applauds an Arkansas Women in Agriculture proclamation — ceremonial/publicity content under negative clarification (3). The reference to her having 'played a key role in the 2002 farm bill debate' is a passing biographical note inside a release whose main purpose is ceremonial recognition; per the boundary rule, that is not sufficient for a '1' label."
  },
  "3482": {
    "document_id": "3482",
    "label": "1",
    "explanation": "Mikulski and Cardin announce a $294,980 federal SAFER grant for Allegany County firefighters, with role language noting Mikulski sits on the appropriations subcommittee funding the program and 'fights each year to increase its federal funding.' Per clarification (1), announcements of federal money benefiting the state — especially with committee-role language — function as credit claims."
  },
  "3577": {
    "document_id": "3577",
    "label": "0",
    "explanation": "Sarbanes's hearing statement is primarily oversight and criticism of FEMA's handling of the flood insurance program plus a list of policy issues to examine. Although he mentions 'reforms that I worked with Senator Bunning... to formulate and move through,' this is a passing reference within a statement whose main purpose is oversight/criticism; under the boundary rule in the negative clarification, that is not sufficient for a '1' label."
  },
  "3659": {
    "document_id": "3659",
    "label": "0",
    "explanation": "This matches the codebook's negative example exactly: Clinton welcomes an advisory board's recommendation and urges the Secretary to adopt it. She claims no role in causing the decision, it is only a recommendation awaiting final approval, and the thrust is advocacy for further action — excluded under negative clarification (5)."
  },
  "3687": {
    "document_id": "3687",
    "label": "1",
    "explanation": "Bingaman announces that New Mexico State University has been awarded $599,691 in USDA funds for food-safety research. Per clarification (1), a senator's announcement of federal grant money benefiting his state functions as a credit claim even without an explicit 'I secured it.'"
  },
  "4208": {
    "document_id": "4208",
    "label": "1",
    "explanation": "The release's primary news is that Dorgan and Lott are introducing their own bipartisan bill (the Media Ownership Act of 2007) with named co-sponsors. Under clarification (3), releases whose primary news is the senator's own legislative product — introducing or sponsoring a bill — are credit-claiming."
  },
  "4585": {
    "document_id": "4585",
    "label": "1",
    "explanation": "Schumer announces over $134,000 in federal fire grants to two Westchester County fire departments (part of nearly $3 million statewide), and the release features his role ('Schumer was among the first senators who recognized the need... to establish a funding program'). This fits clarification (1) on announcing federal money and clarification (5) on featuring past achievements."
  },
  "4771": {
    "document_id": "4771",
    "label": "1",
    "explanation": "This matches the codebook's positive example exactly: Clinton claims she 'played a key role' in preventing the removal of C-130 aircraft from Stratton during the 2005 BRAC round — a retrospective claim of responsibility for a concrete benefit to her state under clarifications (2) and (5)."
  }
}

{
  "817": {
    "document_id": "817",
    "label": "1",
    "explanation": "The release's primary news is Senator Collins's own legislative product: bipartisan legislation 'sponsored by U.S. Senators Susan Collins and Dianne Feinstein' receiving a subcommittee hearing, with Collins testifying in support of 'her legislation.' Under clarification (3), releases whose primary news is the senator's own bill (sponsorship or its advancement) count as credit-claiming; her sponsorship is the featured element rather than a passing biographical reference."
  },
  "871": {
    "document_id": "871",
    "label": "0",
    "explanation": "Salazar questions and criticizes the Administration's budget cuts to PILT, LWCF, and Park Service rules, and references a letter he wrote urging against rule changes. This is oversight/criticism and pure advocacy for future action with no result claimed, falling under negative clarifications (1) (position-taking) and (4) (letters/requests with no achieved outcome). No funding, decision, or legislative result is claimed as his."
  },
  "1118": {
    "document_id": "1118",
    "label": "0",
    "explanation": "Carper's statement explains his vote against the constitutional amendment banning gay marriage. It is a classic vote explanation/position-taking release under negative clarification (1); he claims no responsibility for any government outcome, and his references to past actions as governor concern state law, not a federal benefit he delivered."
  },
  "1177": {
    "document_id": "1177",
    "label": "0",
    "explanation": "Pryor and Chambliss 'call on' the Air Force to reimburse subcontractors, announce they will 'continue to mount pressure,' and are 'working together on a legislative fix.' These are demands and pledges of future action with no result achieved, squarely within negative clarification (4) (pure advocacy). No outcome is claimed as produced by their efforts."
  },
  "1230": {
    "document_id": "1230",
    "label": "1",
    "explanation": "Although Harkin and Hatch 'welcome' an FDA action, they explicitly claim a role in producing it: they were 'the principal authors' of DSHEA, which created the GMP authority, and 'have been pushing the FDA to publish GMPs for more than a decade.' This fits clarification (4) (agency action following the senators' pressure) and (5) (featured recitation of past legislative achievement), distinguishing it from the Clinton-NIOSH negative example where the senator claimed no role."
  },
  "1320": {
    "document_id": "1320",
    "label": "1",
    "explanation": "Headlined 'SEN. HUTCHISON SECURES DROUGHT, WILDFIRE RELIEF FOR TEXAS,' the release announces $3 billion in disaster relief and quotes Hutchison: 'I am pleased to help secure this funding.' This is an explicit first-person claim of responsibility for federal funds under clarification (1) and (2)."
  },
  "1747": {
    "document_id": "1747",
    "label": "1",
    "explanation": "The release announces Senate approval of 'Byrd's provision' boosting funding for the Youth ChalleNGe program and notes he 'worked with the National Guard Bureau to create this program in 1991.' This is the senator's own legislative product advancing (clarification (3)) plus a featured recitation of past achievement (clarification (5))."
  },
  "1862": {
    "document_id": "1862",
    "label": "1",
    "explanation": "Baucus and Tester announce $12.5 million for Butte military projects included in the Defense Appropriations Bill, stating they 'delivered on their promise to secure federal funding' and 'were proud to go to bat for these dollars.' This is a paradigmatic federal-money announcement with explicit responsibility language under clarification (1) and (2)."
  },
  "2026": {
    "document_id": "2026",
    "label": "1",
    "explanation": "The release's primary news is Senate Judiciary Committee passage of the Leahy-Specter Personal Data Privacy and Security Act, legislation 'introduced by Chairman Patrick Leahy.' Committee approval of the senator's own bill is expressly credit-claiming under clarification (3)."
  },
  "2627": {
    "document_id": "2627",
    "label": "1",
    "explanation": "This mirrors the codebook's own positive example: 'SEN. TALENT SECURES $1.5 MILLION' announces that 'the Senate has approved his request' for funding for a University of Missouri facility, with university officials thanking him for securing the money — an explicit claim of responsibility for delivered federal funds under clarification (1) and (2)."
  },
  "2675": {
    "document_id": "2675",
    "label": "0",
    "explanation": "Dodd, Lieberman, and Simmons met with BRAC commissioners to advocate keeping Sub Base New London open. The release stresses the outcome is undecided ('this is only one step in this marathon... until we... hopefully prevail'). This is pure advocacy for future action under negative clarification (4); no decision has been won and no role in a result is claimed."
  },
  "3271": {
    "document_id": "3271",
    "label": "1",
    "explanation": "The release announces unanimous Senate passage of S.2856, 'the measure authored by Idaho Senator Mike Crapo,' and recounts that 'Crapo has led the Senate Banking Committee' through hearings on the bill. Passage of the senator's own authored legislation is credit-claiming under clarification (2) and (3)."
  },
  "3477": {
    "document_id": "3477",
    "label": "1",
    "explanation": "Cornyn's office announces the Senate confirmation of a Texan (Dinkins) to chair the Privacy and Civil Liberties Oversight Board, featuring his role language: he is a Judiciary Committee member who 'chaired the nominations hearing for Raul and Dinkins.' Under clarification (1), announcements of appointments benefiting the senator's state function as credit-claiming when accompanied by role language such as committee membership or a procedural role. I acknowledge some ambiguity, since Cornyn never explicitly says he secured or recommended the nomination, but the bearer-of-news-plus-role structure fits the clarification."
  },
  "3501": {
    "document_id": "3501",
    "label": "0",
    "explanation": "Tester expresses disappointment at the Air Force's plan to eliminate missiles at Malmstrom and pledges to 'continue to fight' and 'work toward getting another mission.' This is position-taking plus a pledge of future advocacy with no result claimed — negative clarifications (1) and (4)."
  },
  "3534": {
    "document_id": "3534",
    "label": "0",
    "explanation": "The release is headlined 'DOMENICI CREDITS PRESIDENT'S GASOLINE PRICE PLAN' and consists of praise for President Bush's announced steps plus a call for unified action. Negative clarification (2) expressly excludes praise directed at others, including crediting the President; Domenici claims no responsibility for any outcome himself."
  },
  "3560": {
    "document_id": "3560",
    "label": "1",
    "explanation": "The Arkansas delegation announces $877,749 in HUD grants to Arkansas public housing agencies, with a detailed list of award amounts. Under clarification (1), announcements of federal grants benefiting the senator's state count as credit-claiming even without an explicit 'I secured it,' because the senators serve as bearers of the news. The quotes applauding local agencies do not change that the release's news is the delivery of federal funds to the state."
  },
  "3729": {
    "document_id": "3729",
    "label": "1",
    "explanation": "Grassley announces the appointment of an interim U.S. Attorney for the Northern District of Iowa and states that Dummermuth 'was one of three names Grassley nominated to fill the opening.' This is an appointment announcement with an explicit claim that the senator's recommendation produced it — clarification (1) (appointments) and (4) (action following the senator's recommendation)."
  },
  "3744": {
    "document_id": "3744",
    "label": "1",
    "explanation": "Inouye serves as bearer of the news that the CDC will establish a field station in Hawaii, and the release features his role: 'Several years ago the [CDC] at Senator Inouye's request established a small occupational health center at the University of Hawaii at Hilo.' Under clarification (1) (agency-decision announcement by the senator) and (5) (featured recitation of a past result produced at his request), this is credit-claiming. Unlike the Clinton-NIOSH negative example, Inouye explicitly claims a causal role in a prior CDC decision benefiting his state."
  },
  "3845": {
    "document_id": "3845",
    "label": "0",
    "explanation": "Bayh's statement responds to OPEC's production cut and urges Congress to pass his energy plan. The primary purpose is position-taking and advocacy for future action (negative clarifications (1) and (4)); no outcome has been achieved. Although he notes he is lead sponsor of S.2025, the bill reference is not the release's news — it functions as support for his advocacy, so under the boundary rule (passing reference inside a position-taking statement) it does not warrant a '1.'"
  },
  "4531": {
    "document_id": "4531",
    "label": "1",
    "explanation": "The release's primary news is that Thune and Clinton 'introduced legislation' extending child care benefits for families of fallen soldiers — the senators' own legislative product, which clarification (3) treats as credit-claiming. It additionally features past achievements (co-sponsoring survivor-benefit increases and traumatic-injury insurance), reinforcing the credit claim under clarification (5)."
  }
}
